---
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

/*
  Kept deliberately specific: it names the actual storage keys, the actual
  retention windows from the backend's config, and the actual columns written.
  If the game's storage changes, this page changes with it — the timing of the
  `_bnet_uid` cookie in particular is described in app/player_id.py in the
  backend repo, and the two are meant to be edited together.
*/
const lastUpdated = "20 September 2026";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What brutenis.net stores, when, and why — in plain terms."
>
  <h1 class="text-5xl font-heading font-bold text-yarn-coral mb-8">Privacy</h1>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The short version: reading this site stores nothing in your browser. The
      server notes the request in a log, with your address truncated first.
      Playing the game stores a little in your browser. Only submitting a score
      to the <a href="/leaderboard">leaderboard</a> creates anything that
      identifies you across visits, and it is one random number.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no advertising, no tracking pixels, no third-party fonts or CDN,
      and no embedded content from anywhere else. No analytics script runs on
      these pages. The only request your browser makes to another company is one
      you make yourself by clicking a link. Visitor counts come from the server's
      own logs, with addresses truncated before they are written — described
      under <a href="#logs">server logs</a> below.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Controller under Art. 4(7) GDPR:<br />
      Brutenis Gliwa<br />
      <a href="mailto:brutenis@gmail.com">brutenis@gmail.com</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      That address is read by a person, and it is the right one for anything on
      this page — a leaderboard entry you want removed, a question about what is
      stored, or a correction to something here that is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
    <p>
      All of it is created by the site itself; none of it is readable by anyone
      else. Because each item is needed for something you asked the site to do,
      none of it requires a consent banner under §25 Abs. 2 TDDDG — which is why
      you are not being shown one.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>theme</code> — whether you picked light or dark. Local to your
        browser, never sent anywhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>attackers-enabled</code> — whether you turned the game on or off.
        Local to your browser, never sent anywhere.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>leaderboard-name</code> — the name you last typed into the submit
        box, so you do not have to type it again. It leaves your browser only if
        you submit a score.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>bnet-session-id</code> and <code>bnet-session-token</code> —
        created the first time you hit a monster. They identify the current run
        so the server can tell that a submitted score belongs to a game it
        actually handed out. Stored in <code>sessionStorage</code>, so they are
        gone when you close the tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>lastSubmittedRank</code> — the position your last submitted score
        reached, so the leaderboard page can highlight your row when it sends you
        there. Also <code>sessionStorage</code>, also gone with the tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>_bnet_uid</code> — a random identifier, stored as a cookie for 90
        days. <strong>It is written only at the moment you submit a score</strong>,
        never on a page view and never for someone who only plays. It is
        <code>HttpOnly</code> (no script on the page can read it) and scoped to
        <code>/api</code>, so it is sent with the game's requests and not with
        ordinary page loads.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why the identifier exists</h2>
    <p>
      Two reasons, both of which are the reason the leaderboard works at all.
      It lets the board show your best run rather than one row per attempt, and
      it lets a submitted score be weighed against what the same player has done
      before, which is most of what stops the board filling up with impossible
      numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is a random number and nothing else. It is not derived from your device,
      your address, or anything about you; it cannot follow you to another site;
      and it is not combined with any other source. Clearing your browser's site
      data for brutenis.net ends it, and the next score you submit starts a new
      one.
    </p>

    <h2>What reaches the server</h2>
    <p>When you start a game, a row is created holding a random session
    identifier, the player identifier described above, and the time.</p>
    <p>When you submit a score, that row also receives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the name you typed — anything you like, including something made up;</li>
      <li>your score, level, and how long the run lasted;</li>
      <li>whether you played on desktop or mobile, and the version of the game;</li>
      <li>
        a log of the run: the kills, level-ups and pickups it consisted of, each
        with a timestamp in milliseconds from the start. This is what the
        anti-cheat check is run against.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The name, score, level, duration, date, weapon and platform are shown
      publicly on the leaderboard. Nothing else is.
    </p>

    <h2>Legal bases</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Everything up to and including starting a game: storage necessary for a
        function you requested, §25 Abs. 2 TDDDG. No separate consent is needed
        and none is asked for.
      </li>
      <li>
        Publishing your chosen name and score on the leaderboard: your consent,
        Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR, given by filling in the name and pressing submit. You
        can withdraw it at any time by asking for the entry to be removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        Checking a submitted run for cheating, and keeping the site available:
        legitimate interests, Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR — an honest leaderboard and a
        working server. Recital 47 names fraud prevention as such an interest.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A game that was started but never submitted: deleted automatically after
        <strong>2 hours</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The run log: deleted automatically after <strong>7 days</strong>. The
        leaderboard entry survives; the behavioural trace behind it does not.
      </li>
      <li>
        The leaderboard entry itself — name, score, level, duration, date: kept
        until you ask for it to be removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>_bnet_uid</code> cookie: 90 days in your browser, restarted
        each time you submit a score.
      </li>
      <li>
        Web server logs: rotated and deleted as they age. The summary built from
        them is kept indefinitely, and holds counts rather than addresses.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="logs">Server logs and hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site runs on a server rented from Hetzner Online GmbH in Germany, which
      acts as a processor under Art. 28 GDPR. Your requests reach that server, so
      Hetzner necessarily handles them in transit; no data is transferred outside
      the EU.
    </p>
    <p>
      The web server keeps an access log, so that I can see roughly how many
      people visit and which pages they read. Each line records the time, the
      page requested and how it was requested, the response status and size, the
      browser's user agent, the page you followed a link from if there was one,
      and the network the request came from.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>That last part is truncated before it is written.</strong> An
      address is cut down to its network block — the last part of an IPv4 address
      is replaced with zero, and an IPv6 address keeps only its site prefix. No
      full address ever reaches this log. What is left is coarse enough that it
      cannot pick out a household, and precise enough to say which country a
      visit came from, which is the only thing it is used for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Those log files are rotated and deleted as they age. What outlasts them is
      a summary — counts of pages, days, browsers and countries — which contains
      no addresses at all. It is not published; it sits behind a password and only
      I can see it. The basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR: knowing whether anyone reads
      the thing, and noticing when the site is being attacked.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no analytics script involved in any of this. Nothing is added to
      the pages, nothing is stored on your device, and nothing is sent to another
      company. It is the server writing down the requests it already had to
      answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The game's API deliberately does <em>not</em> keep a request log of its own;
      it would have recorded full addresses, and the masked log above covers the
      same requests. It does note that a session was created, and the name and
      score of a submitted run. The firewall separately records connections it
      rejects, and those entries do hold the full address they came from — it is
      the one place on the server that keeps one. That is ordinary practice for
      spotting attacks, and it covers connections that were refused rather than
      visits to the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You have the right to access your data (Art. 15), to have it corrected
      (Art. 16) or erased (Art. 17), to restrict its processing (Art. 18), to
      receive it in a portable form (Art. 20), and to object to processing based
      on legitimate interests (Art. 21). Where processing rests on consent, you
      can withdraw that consent at any time without affecting what was lawful
      before.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice, for a leaderboard entry you can do it yourself: your own row
      on the <a href="/leaderboard">leaderboard</a> carries a
      <strong>Remove</strong> button, which deletes every run you have submitted
      and needs no email and no waiting. If that button is not there — most
      likely because you have since cleared your cookies — email the address
      above with the name and score instead. You are also free to complain to a
      data protection supervisory authority, which for a controller in Germany is
      the authority of the federal state they are established in.
    </p>

    <h2>Doing it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      Clearing site data for brutenis.net in your browser's settings removes
      everything described in the first section, including the identifier.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Do it in that order, though: scores first.</strong> The Remove
      button on the leaderboard works by recognising the identifier in your
      browser, so once you have cleared it, neither you nor the server can tell
      which entry was yours, and the only route left is emailing me. Remove the
      entry first, then clear whatever you like.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      This page is versioned alongside the site's source code, so its history is
      public in the <a href="https://github.com/LiquidFun/brutenis.net">repository</a>.
      Last updated: {lastUpdated}.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
